Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the 'ability to consummate' component subject to Material Adverse Effect carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: 
For purposes of this Agreement, "Material Adverse Effect" means any event, change, or circumstance that, individually or in aggregate, materially and adversely affects the business, financial condition, assets, or liabilities of the Company. Notwithstanding this broad definition, the Agreement expressly excludes from being considered a Material Adverse Effect:
(i) any deterioration arising from shifts in general economic conditions or standard industry trends, unless such deterioration is shown to be uniquely severe compared to industry peers;
(ii) any changes in accounting principles or interpretations thereof, irrespective of timing;
(iii) any limitation, delay, or inability in the Company’s ability to consummate the transactions contemplated herein, which shall be treated as an inherent risk of the merger process rather than a Material Adverse Effect. (Refer to Pages 10-12 for complete provisions.)
