Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement:
“Adverse Impact” shall mean any change, event, or effect that, either individually or in aggregate, (i) has caused or would reasonably be expected to cause a material adverse effect on the business, financial condition, operational performance, or results of operations of the Target and its Affiliates, or (ii) would, or is reasonably expected to, delay, impair, or preclude the consummation of the transactions contemplated herein. Notwithstanding the foregoing, no change, event, or effect shall be deemed to constitute an ‘Adverse Impact’ if such change, event, or effect:
   (a) arises solely from general economic or industry-wide fluctuations or typical market conditions;
   (b) results exclusively from temporary delays in satisfying customary closing conditions, when such delays are designed to ensure the prudence of the consummation process;
   (c) is attributable to regulatory or statutory reinterpretations that have been pre-approved in writing by the Parent;
   (d) is limited solely to an adverse effect on the ability to consummate, provided that such effect is due exclusively to adjustments in scheduling or required regulatory review mandated by a Governmental Authority, and such adjustments do not extend beyond 90 days from the agreed closing date.
For purposes of clarity, any impairment affecting the ability to consummate that falls within the parameters of subclause (d) is expressly subject to this carveout, and shall not be treated as constituting an ‘Adverse Impact’ under this Agreement.

