Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by selecting the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement:
"'Material Adverse Effect' means any event, change, or circumstance, whether occurring singly or in aggregate, that (a) materially impairs the financial condition, operations, or prospects of the Company and its Subsidiaries, or (b) materially impedes, delays, or prevents the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated herein. Notwithstanding the foregoing, an impairment of the ability to consummate the merger shall not be deemed to occur if such impairment is solely due to any of the following carveout conditions:
    (i) Volatility in currency exchange rates or minor fluctuations in interest rates affecting general market conditions;
    (ii) Industry-wide downturns that affect all comparable companies in the sector;
    (iii) Government-ordered public health measures or nationwide directives, including, but not limited to, lockdowns or other pandemic-related restrictions;
    (iv) Modifications in generally accepted accounting principles (GAAP) or regulatory reporting requirements subsequent to the signing of this Agreement; and
    (v) Force majeure events, such as natural disasters, unless such events have a clearly disproportionate impact compared to industry peers.
Further, any delay or failure arising from a breach by the Company or its Subsidiaries shall not benefit from these carveout conditions, nor shall any impairment caused by such a breach be subject to exclusion under this definition."

